# The todo stack: a standing "what am I supposed to be doing" anchor,
# tasks pushed onto it and popped off it, and the two lanes that keep a
# task alive until something closes it.
#
# This is POLICY -- how to conduct oneself, what a task is, when it counts
# as done -- and none of that belongs in the engine's own bundle (see
# `ugm/rules/bundle.ugm`'s own header, and `the_bundle()` in
# `selftest.py`, both of which the "load this into the bundle" question
# ran straight into). It ships as an ordinary corpus instead: `load_file`
# it alongside your own, the same way `tools_approval.ugm` is loaded
# alongside a corpus that wants approval-gating -- and with the SAME
# `scope=`, or `task(...)` in each file mints a twin nobody's rules can
# see (`docs/tools-approval.md`).
#
# -- the anchor --------------------------------------------------------
#
# `internal_todo` is always attentioned: pinned (`floor=1`) the moment this
# file loads, in ANY machine, regardless of whether the host corpus ever
# says anything -- `say todo: +boot` is this file's own arrival, not the
# host's, so the pin does not depend on the host corpus's own kickoff.
# Attending is *taking care of something* (§20), and there is always
# exactly one thing this agent is taking care of even when nothing else
# is happening: whatever is on top of its own stack.
say todo: +boot

fact +top(internal_todo, internal_todo)

rule <pin-todo>
  +says(todo, boot)
  no pinned(internal_todo)
->
  +pinned(internal_todo)
=> attend(internal_todo, 5, 1, 1)
fact +lane(<pin-todo>, todo)

# The bootstrap channel is this file's OWN plumbing, never a host corpus's
# business -- `<open-task>` excludes it structurally rather than by racing
# `<pin-todo>` for the first turn (tried and lost: `<open-task>` reacts to
# `arrived(...)` directly, one hop before `<pin-todo>` even has `says(...)`
# to read, so a same-tick pre-emption is always too late).
fact +internal(todo)

# -- opening a task ------------------------------------------------------
#
# System-wide: something arrives on a channel, and answering it becomes an
# open task. Generic over the channel and over what was said -- `+tk` mints
# one task per arrival, guarded by `opened(...)` so a repeated tick asking
# the same question twice does not open it twice. Brushes the arrival back
# -- consuming it here is not the last thing that should happen to it, the
# same reason `<care>` brushes in `delay.ugm`: `<intake>` (the bundle's own
# crossing) wants the SAME occasion, in another lane, later in this same
# round, and consuming is global (§20) -- without the brush, whichever of
# the two runs first is the only one that ever sees it.
#
# Its OWN lane, not `todo` below -- measured, not supposed: `todo` already
# holds five other rules, `SHORTLIST` (`core/attention.py`) only walks five
# candidates before it stops widening, and a sixth ranked last on a busy
# tick is never tried at all that tick -- so two arrivals landing together
# had the second lose its turn forever, the identical shape as `<care>`
# needing its own hop in `delay.ugm`, one lane up.
rule <open-task>
  $arr = arrived($channel, $said)
  no opened($channel, $said)
  no internal($channel)
->
  +opened($channel, $said)
  +task(+tk)
  +about(+tk, $said)
  +via(+tk, $channel)
=> brush($arr)
fact +lane(<open-task>, open)

# `<intake>` (the bundle's own crossing) writes `says(...)` as an ordinary
# move's ordinary conclusion, so it fades at a brush's one tick like
# anything else a rule happens to write -- and this file is BUSY, with a
# `todo`/`brush` lane of its own contending for the same rounds, so a host
# corpus's own `<trust>`-style rule can lose that one tick before its turn
# comes around (measured: `delay.ugm` needed the identical fix once its
# own facts stopped being loaded pre-attended). Re-attends the REAL
# believed node -- a match, never a rebuild -- at the arrival's own
# strength, so a host corpus does not have to know this file is busy to
# write a `<trust>` rule that works.
after <intake> { $sp = says($channel, $said) } => attend($sp, 5, 1, 1)

# Any rule anywhere may open a task the same way -- `+task($t)` is the
# whole contract, not something only `<open-task>` may write. This is what
# notices a NEW one (from `<open-task>` or from a corpus's own rule) and
# gives it a durable place in mind: `+tk` above is a mint marker and
# cannot be attended from the rule that minted it (a postcondition sees
# only the antecedent's own bindings), so pinning happens one tick later,
# off the ordinary belief `task($t)` becomes.
# `keep task($t)` here and everywhere else this file reads it below:
# firing discharges what it matches by default now (docs/design/
# intensity-gates.md), and `task($t)` is a durable record several rules
# read across many ticks -- `<push-task>`, `<brush-top-task>`,
# `<retire-completed-task>`, and this file's own `selftest.py` caller,
# which still expects `task($t)` believed at the end of a run. A plain
# `+` would retire it the first time ANY one of them matched it.
rule <notice-new-task>
  keep task($t)
  no noticed($t)
->
  +noticed($t)
=> attend($t, 5, 1, 1)
fact +lane(<notice-new-task>, todo)

# -- the stack ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One pointer, `top(internal_todo, $t)`, always exactly one live occasion:
# pushing erases the old value and asserts the new in the same move.
# `internal_todo` is its own base case -- the sentinel `fact` above -- so
# pushing the very first task and popping the very last are the same code
# as every push and pop between them, no separate empty/non-empty branch.
rule <push-task>
  keep task($t)
  no under($t, internal_todo)
  +top(internal_todo, $prev)
->
  -top(internal_todo, $prev)
  +top(internal_todo, $t)
  +beneath($t, $prev)
  +under($t, internal_todo)
fact +lane(<push-task>, todo)

# A task marked `completed($t)` does not have to be the CURRENT top to be
# closed -- closing whatever is buried is ordinary business (see `judge`
# below) -- but the STACK only has to answer for what is on top. If the
# top is completed, this pops to what is beneath it, and does so again on
# the next tick if THAT is already completed too: a task closed while
# buried is skipped the moment it is reached, not compacted eagerly.
#
# `keep completed($t)`: read here AND by `<retire-completed-task>` below,
# neither of which is the rule that CONCLUDES it (the host corpus's own
# judge is) -- a plain `+` on either would race the other for the one
# occasion the judge wrote, and whichever lost would never see it.
rule <skip-completed-top>
  +top(internal_todo, $t)
  keep completed($t)
  +beneath($t, $prev)
->
  -top(internal_todo, $t)
  +top(internal_todo, $prev)
fact +lane(<skip-completed-top>, todo)

# Unpin a closed task so it fades (§20) rather than sitting in mind
# forever at the same floor an open task gets.
rule <retire-completed-task>
  keep completed($t)
  keep task($t)
  no retired($t)
->
  +retired($t)
=> attend($t, 1, 1, 0)
fact +lane(<retire-completed-task>, todo)

# -- brushing: the general rule, in its own lane -------------------------
#
# Always brushes the top OPEN task -- re-attends its own two matched
# lines (`$topline`, `$taskline`) every round, which is what lets THIS
# SAME application keep surviving `_attended_first`'s overlap filter tick
# after tick rather than being consumed once and never seen again. That
# is the whole of "keeping the task open ensures that, even with some
# rule repetition, the task gets closed": a corpus's own rules answering
# `about($t, $said)` keep getting a reason to consider $t, for as long as
# it takes them to conclude `completed($t)`, however many times they
# match and fail to close it in between.
#
# `_attended_first`'s overlap filter is gone (docs/design/
# intensity-gates.md): every rule that matches fires, so nothing here is
# defending against a pick any more, and the `brush(...)`/`attend(...)`
# tail below is vestigial -- harmless FOCUS bookkeeping, not load-bearing.
# What IS still load-bearing is `keep` on both lines: `top(...)` and
# `task($t)` are the stack's own pointer and its own record, read here
# without being the rule that should ever retire either.
rule <brush-top-task>
  keep $topline = top(internal_todo, $t)
  keep $taskline = task($t)
  no completed($t)
->
  -brushed($t)
  +brushed($t)
=> brush($topline), brush($taskline), attend($t, 3, 1, 1)
fact +lane(<brush-top-task>, brush)

# -- judging: reserved, not authored here ---------------------------------
#
# A rule that concludes `completed($t)` -- that a task's business is
# actually finished -- stays in the `judge` lane, guarded a turn every
# round for the SAME reason `<trip>`/`<cooldown-tick>`/`<revive>` need
# `watchdog` in `circuit_breaker.ugm`: closing has to compete with
# whatever the corpus's `main` lane is doing, and a lane that only runs
# when nothing else wins never runs at all. This file writes no `judge`
# rules of its own -- what counts as "done" is the host corpus's
# business, the same reason `bundle.ugm` has no policy either -- but
# reserves the lane and its place in the round:
#
#   rule <task-answered>
#     +about($t, $said)
#     +says($channel, $said)
#     +believe($said)
#     no completed($t)
#   ->
#     +completed($t)
#   fact +lane(<task-answered>, judge)
#
fact +lane_order(open, 1)
fact +lane_order(todo, 2)
fact +lane_order(brush, 3)
fact +lane_order(judge, 4)
